   "l": "Coming out: life. Going in: death.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "To come forth is to live; to go back in is to die.",
   "n": "Four characters, two verbs and two nouns, with no grammar between them at all — no subject, no copula, no particle. Every English version of this line is adding something the Chinese withholds.\n\nWang Bi reads the pairs as elliptical: one comes out into the ground of life, one goes in to the ground of death. The chapter bears him out, since it ends on 死地, 'the ground of death'. On that reading the line is about arriving somewhere, not about the span of a life.\n\nIt is also read as plain birth and dying — to come out is to be born, to go back in is to die. The two are not far apart, and the Chinese supports both without preferring either.",
   "p": true
  },
  {
   "t": "生之徒，十有三；\n死之徒，十有三；\n人之生，動之死地，亦十有三。\n夫何故？\n以其生生之厚。\n蓋聞善攝生者，\n陸行不遇兕虎，\n入軍不被甲兵；\n兕無所投其角，\n虎無所措其爪，\n兵無所容其刃。\n夫何故？\n以其無死地。",
