   "l": "Life's company: of ten, three;\ndeath's company: of ten, three;\nthe living among people — they stir, and go to the ground of death — these too, of ten, three.\nNow, what is the reason?\nBecause of the thickness of their making-life-of-life.\nI gather it is said: one good at husbanding life,\ngoing overland, does not meet wild ox or tiger;\nentering an army, does not undergo mail and weapon;\nthe wild ox has nowhere to drive its horn,\nthe tiger nowhere to set its claws,\nthe weapon nowhere to take in its edge.\nNow, what is the reason?\nBecause in him there is no ground of death.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Of life's company, three in ten; of death's company, three in ten; and of those alive who stir and go straight to the ground of death — these too are three in ten. Why so? Because they lay their living on too thick. I gather it is said that one who is good at husbanding life walks overland without meeting wild ox or tiger, and goes in among armies and takes no hurt from their arms: the wild ox finds nowhere to drive its horn, the tiger nowhere to set its claws, the weapon nowhere to lodge its edge. Why so? Because in him there is no ground of death.",
   "n": "十有三 is the famous problem of this chapter. In ordinary classical numeral idiom 有 stands in for 又 'and', so 十有三 is simply 'thirteen' — the same way 十有五年 is 'fifteen years'. Han Feizi, writing in the third century BC and the earliest commentator we have on this book, reads it that way and even names the thirteen: the four limbs and the nine openings of the body. Wang Bi reads it instead as a proportion, three parts out of ten. We follow Wang Bi, who is the primary crib for this text, and the literal keeps the characters showing ('of ten, three') so the other reading stays within reach. Nothing in the text decides it.\n\n之 turns up here doing a third job. In 生之徒 it is the genitive, 'the company of life'. In 動之死地 it is neither genitive nor object pronoun but a full verb, 'to go to': they stir, and go to the ground of death. One character, three grammars, and nothing marking the change.\n\n生生 is the same character twice, first as a verb and then as its own object — to make life of life, to be forever nursing one's own living along. 之厚 makes a noun of the whole and calls it thick. The complaint is not against being alive but against laying life on too heavily, and that thickness is itself the third group's road to the ground of death.\n\n蓋 marks what follows as hearsay: 'I gather', 'it is said'. The author is declining to vouch for the claim he is about to make, and it is worth noticing how carefully the miracle is hedged. 夫 opens both questions and is fú, the particle that says 'now, as for this' — not fū, 'a grown man'. Both questions are rhetorical: the answer follows at once, and both times it comes in under 以, 'because of'.\n\n兕 is a rare character. It is a one-horned wild bovine, generally identified with the rhinoceros; its hide made the best armour of the bronze age, which is why it stands beside the tiger as what a traveller most fears. We render it 'wild ox' and leave the identification open — the single 角 'horn' the beast drives home two lines later is the argument for the rhinoceros. 被 we read as bèi, 'to be subjected to, to suffer', so 不被甲兵 is 'does not undergo arms and armour', i.e. takes no hurt from them. It can also be pī, 'to put on (armour)', which would turn the line into 'he goes in among armies without donning mail' — a soldier's boast rather than a sage's immunity.\n\n無所 before a verb is 'there is nowhere to ——'. Three times over, and the turn of the passage is that the negation is not about the man at all: it is the horn, the claw and the blade that have no place to go. He is not armoured and he is not lucky. He simply presents nothing that death can get hold of — 無死地, no ground of death in him — which answers the chapter's opening line and closes it on the word it began with.\n\nThis unit is long because Wang Bi's commentary treats it as one lemma, and the sense-units of this text are his."
